  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThrough the Week...\n\nThe forecast remains on track: Sprawling high pressure over our \nregion is the primary weather influence heading into the new \nweek. A highly amplified upper level ridge axis is slowly shifting\neast of the Mississippi River, while the axis of the surface high\npressure system over the Northeast US extends along the \nAppalachians and over the Gulf Coast. This subsident, dry weather \nregime with large diurnal temperature ranges will continue through\nat least Thursday, and perhaps longer depending on which longer \nrange guidance best verifies.\n\nIn terms of fire weather danger: Extremely dry vegetation \ncombined with low afternoon RH is the primary driver for the \n\"Elevated\" fire danger graphic. The ridge will be strongest today,\nand then should gradually be suppressed by the westerlies \nthereafter, but do not expect any significant change in sensible \nweather at the surface through at least Thursday. Without any\nappreciable change in the fire danger elements, will keep the fire\ndanger graphic going.\n\nThe Upcoming Weekend... \n\nBy next weekend, the general idea in the guidance is for at least\nsome moist return flow and possible interaction with a \nshortwave/cold front that could bring at least low to medium rain \nchances. The greatest chance for rain at this point looks to be \nSunday. Continue to monitor our updates as we approach the \nweekend and timing/intensity details can be have more certainty \nand more convergent solutions. Regardless of any rain chances, \ntemperatures for the upcoming Halloween day/night may push 10 \ndegrees warmer than normal. /OAJ/EC/",
